ff802-cc-auto
=============

Automatic class-compliant (CC) mode for the original RME Fireface 802
(not the 802 FS). After booting Linux with the unit still in proprietary
USB mode (2a39:3fcd, e.g. after Windows), the unit is prepared for Linux
audio without running "ff802-cc enable" by hand.

The automation is OFF after installation.

  ff802-cc-auto status   state, connected unit, last result (anyone)
  ff802-cc-auto on       turn on  (administrator: pkexec or sudo)
  ff802-cc-auto off      turn off (the active local user, no password)

The state is kept in /var/lib/ff802-cc-auto/mode and survives reboots.

What happens when the automation is on
--------------------------------------

1. udev (70-ff802-cc-auto.rules) sees the unit at boot or when it is
   connected. In proprietary mode (2a39:3fcd) it starts
   ff802-cc-auto-enable@<port>.service, in class-compliant mode
   (0424:3fdd) ff802-cc-auto@<port>:<bus>:<devnum>.service.
   udev itself executes nothing.
2. The service (root without capabilities, no network) reads the cached
   sysfs attributes: USB ID 2a39:3fcd, manufacturer "RME", product
   "Fireface 802 (<serial>)", high speed. Anything else is refused.
3. The connection is recorded in /run/ff802-cc-auto/attempts: at most one
   attempt per connection, also after errors. A reboot or a reconnection
   is a new connection.
4. Right before the write everything is checked again (same connection,
   same device, automation still on). Then the CC enable command
   01 00 44 BD is sent once to interface 1, bulk OUT endpoint 0x0C.
5. Then the USB hub port of exactly this unit is reset, with the same
   code and timing as "ff802-cc enable": disable=1, 2 s, disable=0, and
   up to 15 s waiting until the unit is back at the same port as
   0424:3fdd. The unit gets a new device number; its own udev event
   (ff802-cc-auto@...) confirms the mode ("Fireface 802 -
   Class-Compliant-Modus aktiv") and runs the optional mixer
   initialisation. No reconnecting is needed. The audio configuration of
   ff802-ucm-profile is used unchanged.

The command only sets the TotalMix option "Activate CC mode on
disconnect"; the port reset is that disconnection.

The enable unit may write exactly one file below /sys: the "disable"
attribute of the hub port of this unit
(/sys/bus/usb/devices/<port>/port/disable, ReadWritePaths=); /sys stays
read-only otherwise (ProtectKernelTunables=yes). It has access to USB
device nodes only; ff802-cc-auto@ has access to the ALSA sequencer only.

If the port cannot be reset (port not found, not this unit's, not
writable), the command is still sent and a notification asks you to
disconnect the USB cable briefly and reconnect it ("Class-Compliant-Modus
vorbereitet").

If the reset does not end with the unit back in class-compliant mode
(the port could not be disabled or re-enabled, the unit did not come back
within 15 s, or it came back with another USB ID), nothing is retried:
the notification "automatische Umschaltung nicht bestätigt" asks you to
reconnect the cable, and /run/ff802-cc-auto/reset-blocked stops any
further automatic port reset until the next boot or "ff802-cc-auto on".
The command itself is still sent once per new connection, so a failing
reset can never repeat itself. If the hub port was left disabled, the
journal shows the command to enable it again.

Notifications
-------------

The system service only writes /run/ff802-cc-auto/event (and mixer-event,
see below). The systemd user units ff802-cc-notify.path/.service (started
with every graphical session) show each event once. If nobody was logged in (switch at boot), the
notification is shown at login while it is still relevant. A missing
notification never affects the USB side.

Mixer initialisation (optional, OFF by default)
-----------------------------------------------

Independent of the automation, also for a unit that was switched with
"ff802-cc enable" or is already in CC mode when it is switched on:

  ff802-cc-auto mixer-init status   state and last result (anyone)
  ff802-cc-auto mixer-init on       turn on  (administrator)
  ff802-cc-auto mixer-init off      turn off (the active local user)
  ff802-cc-auto mixer-init dry-run  find unit, sound card and MIDI port; send nothing
  ff802-cc-auto mixer-init apply    initialise once by hand (asks for SEND)

The manual run is /usr/bin/ff802-mixer-init (--list shows the 26
messages); it works for a normal user of the desktop session, takes the
lock of the service and does not depend on "mixer-init on".

The state is kept in /var/lib/ff802-cc-auto/mixer-init. When it is on,
the service sets on EVERY connection of the unit in class-compliant mode
(reconnect, power cycle, boot) exactly these 26 registers of the internal
mixer, in this order, through the ALSA sequencer (MIDI "Port 2" of that
USB connection, found by its sound card; no fixed ALSA numbers):

  Playback 1 -> Phones 9, Playback 2 -> Phones 10   off (0x0000)
  Playback 1..12 -> Hardware Output 1..12           0 dB (0x9000)
  Volume of Hardware Output 1..12                   0 dB (0x0000)
  (Output 1-8 = Analog 1-8, 9-12 = Phones 9-12)

The class-compliant mode routes Playback 1/2 also to Phones 9/10; the
first two writes switch exactly these two crossed routings off (found in
the hardware tests D2/D3). Nothing else is changed: no other crossed
routings, inputs, mute, pan, other outputs or stored setups. Nothing is
read from the unit, so there is no undo: changes made to these 26 values
with TotalMix FX or OSCMix are overwritten at the next connection.

WARNING: 0 dB on the headphone outputs Phones 9-12 can be very loud. Take
headphones off and turn amplifiers down before connecting the unit, and
set the volume in the applications.

Each register is logged (journalctl -t ff802-cc-auto), before them the
configuration "ff802-mixer-init-v2, 26 entries, checksum 0xC97A359C
(verified)". The result is in
/run/ff802-cc-auto/mixer-result (outcome, connection, written=n/26,
config, entries, checksum, MIDI port). If the initialisation fails (MIDI port
not there within 15 s, port not unambiguous, ALSA error, stopped while
sending), nothing is retried and a separate notification "Fireface 802 –
Mixer-Initialisierung fehlgeschlagen" is shown; the class-compliant mode
itself is not affected.

Other crossed routings (e.g. Playback 9 -> Output 10) and input
routings are NOT cleared by the initialisation; only the 26 values above
are set. The messages are sent 10 ms apart over one subscription, then
the program waits 200 ms before closing it.

Verification: play a test signal on one channel of a sink, e.g.
  PIPEWIRE_NODE=<node.name> speaker-test -D pipewire -c 2 -s 1 -t sine -l 1
(node.name from "wpctl inspect <sink id>"); it must be heard on the left
output of that pair only, -s 2 on the right only.

Automatic and manual switching
------------------------------

Automatic (this package, ff802-cc-auto): only proprietary -> class-
compliant, when the unit is connected or present at boot and the
automation is on. It never switches back; after the command it resets the
USB hub port of the unit like "ff802-cc enable" (see above).

Manual (package ff802-cc, NOT part of this package and not required by
it; run with sudo):

  sudo ff802-cc enable    proprietary -> class-compliant, then resets the
                          USB hub port of the unit (no reconnecting)
  sudo ff802-cc disable   class-compliant -> proprietary (USB-MIDI
                          interface 3); refused while the automation is on

To stay in proprietary mode on Linux, turn the automation off first
(ff802-cc-auto off), then use "ff802-cc disable". Otherwise the
automation switches the unit back to class-compliant mode at the next
connection. Without Linux the way back is TotalMix FX on Windows/macOS,
which resets the unit to normal mode, or the optional ARC (hold Prog and
B (Mono) while switching the unit on).

Diagnosis
---------

  ff802-cc-auto status
  journalctl -t ff802-cc-auto -b             system service
  journalctl --user -t ff802-cc-notify -b    notifications
  systemctl list-units --all 'ff802-cc-auto@*' 'ff802-cc-auto-enable@*'

Exit codes of the service: 0 off / handled / CC mode / sent / switched /
mixer done, 3 nothing sent (refused or failed before the write; also a mixer
initialisation that sent nothing), 4 the write failed (device state
unknown: power-cycle the unit and check with "ff802-cc-auto status"; also
a mixer initialisation that stopped while sending), 5 the command was
sent, but the port reset did not end in class-compliant mode (reconnect
the cable; see the journal).

Removal
-------

  ff802-cc-auto off                (optional: stops further switches now)
  sudo apt purge ff802-cc-auto     removes everything incl. the state

Removal deletes /var/lib/ff802-cc-auto and /run/ff802-cc-auto, so a later
installation starts with the automation off. The USB mode of the unit and
the ff802-ucm-profile package are not touched.
